"1. AIDS. 2025 Nov 15;39(14):2059-2063. doi: 10.1097/QAD.0000000000004309. Epub\n2025  Jul 23.\n\nBlood pressure, weight change and incident hypertension after switching to \ndolutegravir in treatment-experienced people with HIV.\n\nSteffen HM(1)(2)(3), Viola E(4), Thawani A(4), Kachere LG(4), Huwa J(4), \nWallrauch C(4)(5), Kasper P(1), Neuhann F(6)(7), Heller T(4)(8), Winkler V(6), \nRambiki E(4), Mahanani MR(6).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)University of Cologne, Faculty of Medicine and University Hospital Cologne, \nClinic for Gastroenterology and Hepatology and.\n(2)Hypertension Center, Cologne, Germany.\n(3)Department of Postgraduate Studies and Research, Chreso University, Lusaka, \nZambia.\n(4)Lighthouse Clinic, Lilongwe, Malawi.\n(5)Institute of Infectious Diseases and Tropical Medicine, LMU University \nHospital, LMU Munich, Munich.\n(6)Institute for Global Health, University Heidelberg, Heidelberg, Germany.\n(7)School of Medicine and Clinical Sciences, Levy Mwanawasa Medical University, \nLusaka, Zambia.\n(8)International Training and Education Center for Health, University of \nWashington, Seattle, USA.\n\nOBJECTIVE: Dolutegravir (DTG) is recommended in WHO guidelines and has \nreportedly been associated with excess gain in body weight (BW).\nDESIGN: In the prospective LighTen Cohort Study (ClinicalTrials.gov NCT02381275) \nwe evaluated changes in BW and blood pressure (BP) of people with HIV (PWH) who \nhad initiated tenofovir (TDF)/lamivudine (3TC)/efavirenz (EFV; TLE). Follow-up \ncontinued after participants had been switched to TDF/3TC/DTG (TLD) in 2019.\nMETHODS: From the electronic medical record system, we retrieved data on BW and \nsystolic BP of LighTen Cohort Study participants (age \u226518\u200ayears), who were still \nin care. We modelled BW and systolic BP over time utilizing interrupted time \nseries (ITS) considering repeated measurements and adjusting for age and sex. \nIncident hypertension was defined as office BP \u2265140/90\u200ammHg using \u22652 \nmeasurements on \u22652 consecutive visits.\nRESULTS: Data of 543 PWH (317 females) were analysed. ITS showed an increase in \nBW of 1.24\u200akg/year on TLE with a minimal contribution (36.5\u200ag/year) during DTG \ntreatment, in contrast to the change in systolic BP: 0.33mmHg/year on TLE vs. \n2.63 mmHg/year on DTG. Incident hypertension in previously normotensive PWH was \nconfirmed in 5.4% (while on TLE) vs. 11.0% (while on TLD).\nCONCLUSIONS: Following the initial increase in BW on TLE, a larger increase in \nBP and a higher risk of incident hypertension was associated with switching \nantiretroviral therapy from TLE to TLD. Focussing on BP control while on DTG and \ntimely initiation of antihypertensive drug therapy can help to reduce the burden \nof hypertension in PWH with their inherent increased cardiovascular risk.\n\nCopyright \u00a9 2025 Wolters Kluwer Health, Inc. All rights reserved.\n\nDOI: 10.1097/QAD.0000000000004309\nPMID: 40704988 [Indexed for MEDLINE]"